<edge source="2D_CWT_SCALOGRAM_3c540e7a" target="2D_CWT_SCALOGRAM_3c540e7a" id="memorize_finding(finding=Consolidated summary at step 10 (for th...)">
  <data key="d3">memorize_finding(finding=Consolidated summary at step 10 (for th...)</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]</data>
  <data key="d6">[[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]]</data>
  <data key="d7">{"finding": "Consolidated summary at step 10 (for the last block of research):\n### \ud83c\udfaf Strategic Lesson\nPeak accuracy in the current search space is largely an **overfitting artifact**, not evidence of robust representation. The initial sweep successfully identifies failure zones (undercapacity, high LR, instability), but univariate correlations have mistaken memorization for generalization and diagnostic outcomes for tunable inputs. The next optimization phase must pivot to **constraint-aware, multivariate tuning** that targets the accuracy-latency Pareto frontier and enforces strict generalization ceilings.\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 What to Keep (Validated Signals)\n* **Failure Triad is Reliable:** Undercapacity (`\u2264~45k params`), elevated learning rates (`&gt;0.001`), and low convergence stability reliably predict trial failure.\n* **Lower LR Advantage:** Consistently anchors better convergence; maintain as a bounded search prior (e.g., `1e-4` to `3e-4`).\n* **Efficiency Trade-off Exists:** `params_count` \u2194 `inference_time_ms` scaling is real and must be tracked as a first-class objective, not a post-hoc metric.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd04 What to Change (Methodological &amp; Priority Shifts)\n| Area | Correction |\n|:---|:---|\n| **Statistical Rigor** | Replace `p=0.0` truncations with exact values/confidence intervals. Apply FDR correction. Use ANOVA/Point-biserial for binary flags (`batch_norm`, `partitioning`). Acknowledge `n` limitations and avoid declaring `p&gt;0.05` as \"no effect\" without power analysis. |\n| **Metric Reframing** | Reclassify `learning_speed` &amp; `convergence_stability` as **early-training diagnostics**, not hyperparameters to optimize. Use them for dynamic early stopping or LR scheduling, not search priors. |\n| **Architecture Assumption** | Drop linear \"deeper/larger = better\" logic. Cap parameter ceilings. Mandate modern primitives: residual paths, proper norm placement, depthwise separable convs, and aggressive dense-layer pruning. |\n| **Overfitting Tolerance** | Treat the `13% train-val gap` as a **hard exclusion constraint**, not a footnote. Cap allowable gap at `\u22643-5%` or apply a regularization penalty in the selection objective. |\n| **Search Strategy** | Replace univariate screening with **Bayesian/response-surface optimization** that explicitly models interactions (`LR\u00d7batch_size`, `depth\u00d7dropout`, `kernel\u00d7stride`). |\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd2e New Hypothesis (Next-Phase Test)\n*If we constrain model capacity to eliminate memorization, hard-code modern normalization/regularization protocols, and optimize via a multivariate search targeting the accuracy-latency Pareto frontier, we will produce models with a `\u22645% generalization gap` and stable convergence. Absolute peak accuracy may drop by `1-2%`, but deployment readiness, out-of-distribution robustness, and inference efficiency will increase measurably.*\n\n---\n\n### \u2699\ufe0f Execution Directives\n1. **Redesign Search Space:** Implement soft/hard parameter caps, remove redundant dense-unit sweeps, and embed residual normalization blocks as baseline.\n2. **Generalization-First Objective:** Switch from maximizing `mean_accuracy` to maximizing `Accuracy - \u03b1*(Train_Val_Gap)^2 - \u03b2*Inference_Latency`.\n3. **Multivariate Tuning Engine:** Deploy a Bayesian optimizer (e.g., Optuna/TPE) with interaction priors. Log `p-values`, `CIs`, and `n` rigorously. Apply FDR to all post-hoc analyses.\n4. **Dynamic Early Stopping:** Replace fixed patience with a **divergence trigger**: halt if `(train_acc - val_acc) &gt; threshold` for `\u2265N` epochs.\n5. **Pareto Reporting:** Every optimization cycle concludes with an accuracy-vs-latency frontier plot. Selection is made from the frontier, not the absolute maximum.", "experiment_name": "2D_CWT_SCALOGRAM", "_data_source": "data_paper_mine_csv", "_manifest_name": "data_paper_mine_csv", "_dataset_params": {"n_fft": 256, "hop_length": 64, "gaf_image_size": 128, "video_num_segments": 10, "video_gaf_image_size": 128, "representation_type": "2D_CWT_SCALOGRAM"}}</data>
  <data key="d8">[-0.05450000000000001, 0.030926012277603155, 0.0001394967328418393, 0.6957907676696777, 0.021006889343261722]</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2D_CWT_SCALOGRAM_3c540e7a" target="2D_CWT_SCALOGRAM_3c540e7a" id="memorize_finding(finding=Consolidated summary at step 11 (for th...)">
  <data key="d3">memorize_finding(finding=Consolidated summary at step 11 (for th...)</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]</data>
  <data key="d6">[[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]]</data>
  <data key="d7">{"finding": "Consolidated summary at step 11 (for the last block of research):\n### \ud83c\udfaf Strategic Lesson\n**Capacity and Learning Rate are reliable first-order signals for accuracy, but treating them in isolation via bivariate correlations masks critical interaction effects, drives compute bloat, and dangerously underestimates generalization risk. The next optimization phase must pivot from brute-force scaling to constrained, interaction-aware modeling where architecture, training dynamics, and regularization are tuned jointly for Pareto efficiency.**\n\n---\n\n#### \u2705 WHAT TO KEEP\n- **First-Order Heuristics:** Architectural depth/width (`num_layers`, `filters`) and lower base learning rates remain the strongest empirical predictors of high accuracy.\n- **Failure Tripwires:** Premature early stopping, elevated final validation loss, and low convergence stability remain reliable indicators of suboptimal configurations.\n- **Compute-Aware Lens:** Acknowledge that accuracy historically scales with parameter count and inference latency, but elevate this correlation into a formal efficiency constraint rather than an inevitable trade-off.\n\n#### \ud83d\udd04 WHAT TO CHANGE\n- **From Bivariate \u2192 Multivariate Analysis:** Replace isolated Pearson `r` with partial correlations, regularized regression, or SHAP/PDP plots. Explicitly model interaction terms (`depth \u00d7 LR`, `kernel_size \u00d7 padding`, `filters \u00d7 batch_norm`).\n- **Stop Dismissing Regularization:** `p &gt; 0.05` does not equal \"neutral.\" Expand search ranges for `dropout`/`l2_lambda`, enforce correct architectural placement, and track the raw **train-test accuracy gap** directly.\n- **Clarify Training Dynamics:** Replace static `epochs` and `p=0.0` with precise metrics: actual epochs vs. patience, explicit LR schedules (warmup/decay), and logged gradient norms. Differentiate `max_epochs` from early stopping triggers.\n- **Adopt Pareto Optimization:** Shift objective function from raw accuracy to **efficiency ratios** (e.g., `accuracy/inference_ms`, `accuracy/model_size`). Require dataset size, augmentation, and split documentation for all evaluations to ground correlations in task context.\n\n#### \ud83e\uddea NEW HYPOTHESIS\n&gt; **Performance will be maximized not by unconstrained capacity scaling, but by operating in a *Regulated Capacity Regime* where depth/width is explicitly coupled with adaptive LR schedules and layer-targeted regularization. In this balanced state, the 13% train-test generalization gap will contract to &lt;6%, while Pareto efficiency (accuracy-per-compute-unit) will improve by \u226530%, proving that structured optimization outperforms brute-force width/depth expansion.**\n\n---\n\n#### \ud83d\udccb Phase 1 Directive\n1. **Design a factorial sweep** testing `depth \u00d7 LR` and `filters \u00d7 dropout` with fixed baselines for other vars.\n2. **Replace static LR** with cosine decay or step scheduling; log gradient norms and validation plateau triggers every epoch.\n3. **Track Pareto fronts** in real-time; auto-prune trials that exceed compute thresholds without proportional accuracy gains.\n4. **Validate on a held-out OOD subset** to stress-test the shrinking generalization gap.\n\n*Proceed to experimental design. Treat prior findings as observational heuristics, not causal rules.*", "experiment_name": "2D_CWT_SCALOGRAM", "_data_source": "data_paper_mine_csv", "_manifest_name": "data_paper_mine_csv", "_dataset_params": {"n_fft": 256, "hop_length": 64, "gaf_image_size": 128, "video_num_segments": 10, "video_gaf_image_size": 128, "representation_type": "2D_CWT_SCALOGRAM"}}</data>
  <data key="d8">[-0.05450000000000001, 0.030926012277603155, 0.0001394967328418393, 0.6957907676696777, 0.021006889343261722]</data>
</edge>
